[{"text":"$d_r\\colon E_r(n,s,c)\\to E_r(n-1,\\,s+r,\\,c+1)$; on each page $r=2,3,\\dots$ the maximal rank the dimensions allow is taken. The pages are processed in order.","section":"The rule as we understood it"},{"text":"The grammar gives $\\text{stem}=4\\sum(\\text{indices})-\\#\\lambda$, that is, $c\\equiv \\text{stem}+s \\pmod 4$. Hence an arrow “colour $+1$” is possible only for $r\\equiv2\\pmod4$: $d_2, d_6, d_{10},\\dots$ The variant “colour $-1$” gives no differentials at all, and in it $\\pi_{44}$ keeps four classes, whereas the lecture says it is empty.","section":"The rule as we understood it"},{"text":"On page $r$ the possible arrows line up into chains $x\\to y\\to z\\to\\cdots$ with decreasing $n$. The maximal total rank on a chain is determined uniquely, but which classes die is not: a middle class can be either a target or a source. “low” fills a chain starting from the end with the smaller $n$, “high” from the end with the larger. For $n\\le149$ the two variants differ only here:","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"only in “low”:","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"from (s, c) · to (s, c) · rank","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{42}$ (7, 2) · $\\pi_{41}$ (9, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{52}$ (9, 2) · $\\pi_{51}$ (11, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{62}$ (11, 2) · $\\pi_{61}$ (13, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{72}$ (13, 2) · $\\pi_{71}$ (15, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{75}$ (6, 2) · $\\pi_{74}$ (8, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{78}$ (7, 2) · $\\pi_{77}$ (9, 3) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{79}$ (7, 3) · $\\pi_{78}$ (9, 4) · 1","section":"Where the rule is ambiguous"},{"text":"$d_{2}$ · $\\pi_{82}$ (15, 2) · $\\pi_{81}$ (17